Collabora Online docker

This directory contains everything needed to compile a
working docker container with Collabora Online.

Docker image can be built from packages or from source code.

All docker images built from this directory can be run with a TZ environment variable
to set the server timezone to something other than UTC.

The Dockerfile uses the --mount parameter of RUN command. Actually it is used for
building  Collabora Online images for Collabora Partners/Customers, who have the secret
URL of supported packages. The --mount parameter requires BuildKit.

   export DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1

Examples:

1. Build latest CODE

   cd from-packages
   docker build --no-cache -t mydomain/code .

2. Build latest CODE 100% open source

The brand package of CODE is not open source. To build an image without it:

   cd from-packages
   docker build --no-cache --build-arg nobrand=yes -t mydomain/code .

3. Build latest CODE with server-side Python macro support

The pyuno and Python script provider packages are not installed by default,
so server-side Python macros (e.g. the CallPythonScript postMessage API) do
not work. The image has no shell or package manager, so they cannot be added
to a running container; build them in instead:

   cd from-packages
   docker build --no-cache --build-arg withpython=yes -t mydomain/code .

Macro execution must also be enabled at runtime, e.g.
extra_params=--o:security.enable_macros_execution=true

4. Build for a platform the hardened base image does not support

The default runtime base (ZenDiS) is published for amd64 and arm64 only. For
other platforms, e.g. ppc64le, override the runtime base with an image that
supports them. Google's distroless base is a good choice: like ZenDiS it
contains only glibc, openssl, ca-certificates and timezone data - no shell,
no package manager - and it is published for ppc64le, s390x and riscv64 too.
Pick the Debian release matching the builder stage (debian:stable-slim):

   cd from-packages
   docker build --no-cache --platform linux/ppc64le \
       --build-arg runtime_base=gcr.io/distroless/base-debian13:latest \
       -t mydomain/code .

Plain debian:stable-slim also works as runtime_base, but it is not
distroless: it ships a shell and a package manager.

5. Build Collabora Online

   cd from-packages
   # Get your secret URL key from https://support.collaboraoffice.com/ (Collabora Partners/Customers)
   # e.g. the secret key is Example-413539ece39485afc35b4a469adfde0a279d2fd2
   echo Example-413539ece39485afc35b4a469adfde0a279d2fd2 > secret_key
   docker build --no-cache --secret id=secret_key,src=secret_key --build-arg type=cool -t mydomain/cool .

6. Build Collabora Online license key enabled version

   cd from-packages
   docker build --no-cache --build-arg type=key -t mydomain/cool .

7. Build Collabora Online from master branch (from source code)

   cd from-source
   ./build.sh

Check build.sh for more build options!


8. SBOM and publishing (from-packages)

Every from-packages build generates image-level SBOMs into the image at
/usr/share/sbom/: collabora-online.cdx.json (CycloneDX 1.6, the format the
ZenDiS / openCode ecosystem consumes) and collabora-online-image-sbom.spdx.json
(an SPDX 3.0.1 aggregate). They merge the base image's own SBOM, one pkg:deb
component per shipped package, and references (path + sha256) to the rich
per-package SPDX 3.0.1 documents inside the image: the engine's
/opt/collaboraoffice*/<package>-sbom.spdx.json and online's
/usr/share/coolwsd/collabora-online-sbom.spdx.json. The Collabora-published
packages are deliberately purl'd as pkg:deb/collabora/... - no distro security
feed covers them - and their component inventory (statically linked C++
externals with CPEs, shipped npm packages with purls) is lifted out of those
SPDX documents into the CycloneDX components list, so one scan of the
image-level SBOM covers the contents of COOL itself, not just the deb layer:

   trivy sbom collabora-online.cdx.json    matches the Debian packages and
                                           the npm purls (GHSA/OSV)
   grype sbom:collabora-online.cdx.json    additionally matches the C++
                                           externals by CPE (NVD)

CPE matching of the statically linked libraries will report CVEs that the
engine has already fixed by backporting patches without a version bump - that
is expected, and correcting it downstream is exactly what the VEX is for.

Build args:

   sbom_base=fetch    (default) pull the base SBOM from the registry
                      attestation, fall back to probing the base's dpkg data
   sbom_base=file     use a base-sbom*.cdx.json from the build context
                      (air-gapped builds)
   sbom_base=probe    for debian / gcr.io distroless base overrides
   sbom_base=none     record the base SBOM as unavailable
   sbom_strict=1      fail the build instead of degrading (release builds)

Publishing, the same way ZenDiS publishes the hardened base images (cosign
signature plus SBOM / VEX / scan / provenance attestations as sidecar tags on
every per-platform digest):

   docker push ... (or let publish.sh push)
   COSIGN_KEY=... ./from-packages/scripts/publish.sh <image-ref>...

This is registry-agnostic (Docker Hub, registry.opencode.de, private
registries). The signing key is the private counterpart of docker/cosign.pub
and lives outside this repository; from-packages/secret_key is the apt
repository secret, never a signing key. VEX statements are maintained in
from-packages/vex/cool.vex.json: publish.sh (with trivy installed) scans the
SBOM and appends new findings as in_triage; update their analysis state as
triage concludes - notably not_affected/fixed for CVEs already patched in the
engine's bundled libraries, which version-based matching cannot see.

Consumers verify with:

   cosign verify --key docker/cosign.pub <image-ref>
   cosign verify-attestation --key docker/cosign.pub --type cyclonedx <image-ref>

The base image's own SBOM and VEX are published by ZenDiS, see
https://gitlab.opencode.de/oci-community/images/zendis/base


Usage

On using the pre-made or self-built Docker images, see Collabora Online SDK:

- https://sdk.collaboraonline.com/docs/installation/Docker_image.html

- https://sdk.collaboraonline.com/docs/installation/CODE_Docker_image.html


Runtime

At runtime use the provided `cool-seccomp-profile.json` as seccomp profile (Ref: https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/seccomp/)
It improves security and performance, allowing the coolwsd process to better containerize documents processes:

   docker run --security-opt cool-seccomp-profile.json
